<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kathiri Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1395 CE – 1967 CE (approx.) · capital Seiyun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sultanate of the Kathiri tribe in Wadi Hadramawt, which ruled Seiyun and Tarim for centuries and was absorbed into South Yemen in 1967.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kathiri tribe rose in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a> in the late 14th and 15th centuries, taking control of the valley's towns. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badr-abu-tuwayriq">Badr Abu Tuwayriq</a> expanded Kathiri rule across Hadramawt and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a>, using firearms and troops obtained through Ottoman contacts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kathiri sultans ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiyun">Seiyun</a>, whose mud-brick palace still dominates the town, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-yemen">Tarim</a>, the religious centre of the valley's sayyid families and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> scholarship. Their power depended on tribal alliances and later on remittances from Hadrami emigrants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. In the 19th century they lost the coast and much of the valley to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>An agreement of 1918 with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> placed the sultanate within the British sphere, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>. In 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> took over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hadramawt">Hadramawt</a>, abolished the sultanate and incorporated it into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kathiri-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kathiri Sultanate" width="320" height="165"><figcaption>Image: Jaume Ollé; derivative work by Malyszkz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathiri_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1395 CE – 1967 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seiyun</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Badr Abu Tuwayriq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kathiri State of Seiyun, Kathiri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1681&amp;focus=kathiri-sultanate" aria-label="Kathiri Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6010 1584 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kathiri Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6330 1840L6295 1853M6330 1840L6207 1863M6330 1840L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6295 1853h0M6207 1863h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6330 1840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6330 1840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Seiyun. <a href="/atlas?year=1681&amp;focus=kathiri-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
